# Global Rules

## User Questions — ALWAYS Use `ask` Tool

- NEVER ask questions as plain text in the chat. ALWAYS use the `ask` tool.
- When choosing between approaches → `ask` with single-select options (`multi: false`)
- When gathering multiple preferences → `ask` with tabbed multi-question interface
- When confirming actions → `ask` with Yes/No options
- When you need free-form input → `ask` with no options (user types their answer)
- Provide clear, actionable options when available
- Include a `context` field to guide the question

## CLI Tool Preferences

When using `bash`, prefer modern CLI tools over legacy ones:
- `rg` instead of `grep` (ripgrep — faster, respects .gitignore)
- `fd` instead of `find` (fd-find — faster, saner defaults)
- `sd` instead of `sed` (simpler syntax, no escaping hell)
- NEVER use `grep`, `find`, or `sed` in bash commands

## Writing Style

- NEVER use the em dash character (—) in any output: chat, docs, commits, comments, PR bodies. Use a plain hyphen, comma, colon, or restructure the sentence.
- Chat replies are terse. Never large paragraphs or long bullet lists.
- Trivial explanations: under 300 words in chat. Anything longer is nontrivial by definition.

## Explanations & Briefs

- Nontrivial explanations (investigations, designs, root causes, proposals, reviews) go in an annotatable HTML brief, NOT chat. Read the `html-brief` skill and follow it.
- Brief structure: TLDR, Context, Problem, Solution. Mermaid or animated diagrams carry the content; prose is minimal framing only.
- Caveats and unresearched items: one minimal list at the end of the brief. No paragraphs about them.
- After building a brief: serve it, start the annotate bridge, and reply in chat with just the URL + up to 3 lines.

## PR Descriptions

- Sections: `## Problem`, `## Solution`, optionally `## Tophat`. Nothing else.
- The code explains the PR; the description only explains the problem and the solution.
- Problem and Solution: one mermaid diagram each, at most one compact table, barely any prose.
- Tophat: 2-4 copy-paste commands, one short comment line max. No narrated validation.
- No changelog sections, evidence essays, implementation history, or AI confidence scores.
- Always read the current description before editing; preserve user trims; write minimal the FIRST time.
- Mermaid on GitHub: avoid `[]` inside node labels (breaks parsing).

## Code Style

- Be concise and direct
- Early exit/return patterns, assert preconditions, flat code over nesting
- Conventional Commits format for commit messages
- Explain what you're about to do before doing it
- When making multiple changes, commit frequently with focused messages
- The user's shell is fish: when giving the user a command to run, show both fish and bash equivalents (or one if identical)
- For your own command execution (bash tool), always use bash syntax
- Never add comments when removing code
- Don't add explanatory comments unless asked
